= To Start Play

#fig(fig-panel())

- Turn on your TV, set its volume low, and tune it to *channel 3*, just as
  for any Atari game. (If your console is connected some other way, use the
  input it always uses.)
- If your TV is black and white, slide the *TV TYPE* switch to *B·W*.
  Otherwise leave it at *COLOR*.
- Slide the *POWER* switch *ON*. The FujiNet menu appears.

The first time you turn it on, FujiNet looks for WiFi networks and asks you to
choose yours. That is the next section. After that, it remembers, and goes
straight to the list of places where games are kept (Section 7).

== The Joystick in the FujiNet Menu

FujiNet's menus are twelve letters wide and need only the joystick and two
switches. They all work the same way:

#controls(title: [In every FujiNet menu],
  [*>*], [The arrow at the left marks the line you are on.],
  [#stick("ud")], [Move up and down the list.],
  [#stick("lr")], [Turn the page, when a list is longer than the screen.],
  [#FIRE], [Choose the line you are on.],
  [#SELECT], [Open the *ACTIONS* menu for this screen: a short list of
    the things you can do here. GAME SELECT again closes it without choosing.],
)

The menu does not use the GAME RESET switch, the difficulty switches or the
TV TYPE switch. The game you load decides what those do.

#note[The *RESET button on the cartridge* is not the console's GAME RESET
switch. GAME RESET belongs to the game. The cartridge's RESET always brings
you back to the FujiNet menu.]

== Hold the Joystick Right Way Up

The red button goes at the top left, away from you, with the cord leading
toward the TV -- the way it sits naturally in your hand.
